<GuideLayout
  title="Scoring System"
  description="How a record's risk score and risk level are calculated."
>
  <p>
    Every record carries a numeric <strong>score</strong> and a
    <strong>risk level</strong> derived from it. The score accumulates points
    from specific, named findings across the eight registries — it is not a
    black box, and every point is traceable to a source.
  </p>

  <h2>Risk levels</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Level</th><th>Score range</th><th>Read as</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>LOW</td><td>0 – 2</td><td>No material findings across the checked sources.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MEDIUM</td><td>3 – 7</td><td>Minor or unconfirmed findings — worth a manual glance.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>HIGH</td><td>8 – 15</td><td>At least one substantive finding — read the full record before retention.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CRITICAL</td><td>16+</td><td>Multiple or severe findings — retractions, license action, or both.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What feeds the score</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Retractions</strong> — a retracted paper is the single heaviest-weighted signal.</li>
    <li><strong>Licensure</strong> — a suspended or revoked license (medical board, state bar, PE license, FINRA/SEC status, depending on industry).</li>
    <li><strong>Litigation history</strong> — prior MDL/JPML appearances or repeated case involvement in the same subject matter.</li>
    <li><strong>Funding disclosures</strong> — NIH or grant funding that overlaps with the subject of testimony, which can raise disclosure questions even without misconduct.</li>
    <li><strong>Publication record</strong> — citation count and publication volume, used as context rather than a penalty.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What it does not do</h2>
  <p>
    The score does not read sentiment, does not weigh case outcomes, and does
    not penalize an expert for having testified before. It only reflects
    what the eight registries return for that name at the time of the last
    data refresh — see <a href="/guide/data-sources/">Data Sources</a> for
    what each one covers, and <a href="/guide/how-to-use/">How To Use</a> for
    how to treat a flagged record.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
